'''Tau Ceti''' is a single [[star]] in the constellation [[Cetus (constellation)|Cetus]]. Its name is a [[Bayer designation]]. [[Stellar classification|Spectrally]], this star is similar to the [[Sun]], although it has only about 78% of the [[Solar mass|Sun's mass]]. At a distance of just under {{convert|12|ly|pc|abbr=off|lk=on}} from the [[Solar System]], it is a [[List of nearest stars|relatively nearby star]] and the closest solitary [[G-type main-sequence star|G-type]] star. The star appears stable, with little [[stellar variation]], and is [[metallicity|metal-deficient]] (low in elements other than hydrogen and helium) relative to the Sun.

It can be seen with the unaided eye with an [[apparent magnitude]] of 3.5.<ref name="aaa494"/> As seen from Tau Ceti, the Sun would be in the northern hemisphere constellation [[Boötes]] with an apparent magnitude of about 2.6.<ref group="nb">From Tau Ceti the Sun would appear on the diametrically opposite side of the sky at the coordinates RA&nbsp;=&nbsp;{{RA|13|44|04}}, Dec&nbsp;=&nbsp;{{DEC|15|56|14}}, which is located near [[Tau Boötis]]. The absolute magnitude of the Sun is 4.8, so, at a distance of {{val|3.65|ul=parsec}}, the Sun would have an apparent magnitude <math>m = M_v + 5 \cdot (\log_{10} 3.64 - 1) = 2.6</math>.</ref><ref>{{citation | title=Allen's Astrophysical Quantities | page=382 | edition=Fourth | editor1-first=Arthur N. | editor1-last=Cox | publisher=Springer | isbn=0-387-95189-X | postscript=. | date=2001-04-20 }}</ref>

Observations have detected more than ten times as much dust surrounding Tau Ceti as is present in the Solar System. Tau Ceti has been an object of interest for [[exoplanet]] searches, and a number of candidate planets have been proposed,<ref name="Feng2017"/> but {{as of|2025|lc=y}} there remains no unambiguous evidence of planets.<ref name=Figueira2025/> Because of its [[debris disk]], any planet orbiting Tau Ceti would face far more [[impact event]]s than present day Earth. Despite this hurdle to [[Planetary habitability|habitability]], its [[solar analog]] (Sun-like) characteristics have led to widespread interest in the star. Given its stability, similarity and relative proximity to the Sun, Tau Ceti is consistently listed as a target for the [[search for extraterrestrial intelligence]] (SETI).<ref name=rutkowski2010>{{citation
 | first1=Chris A. | last1=Rutkowski | title=The Big Book of UFOs
 | publisher=Dundurn | date=2010 | isbn=978-1554887606 | page=33
 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=cqmizCB7iacC&pg=PA33 }}</ref>

==Name==
The name "Tau Ceti" is the [[Bayer designation]] for this star, established in 1603 as part of German celestial cartographer [[Johann Bayer]]'s ''[[Uranometria]]'' star catalogue: it is "number T" in Bayer's sequence of constellation Cetus. '''τ''' is the Greek letter [[Tau (letter)|tau]] (ταῦ), while ''Ceti'' is the Latin genitive form of Cetus. The designation '''τ Ceti''' is therefore [[Romanization of Greek|Latinized]] as '''Tau Ceti''' and abbreviated Tau Cet or τ Cet.

In the catalogue of stars in the ''Calendarium'' of [[Al Achsasi al Mouakket]], written at [[Cairo]] about 1650, this star was designated ''Thālith al Naʽāmāt'' (ثالث النعامات – ''thālith al-naʽāmāt''), which was translated into [[Latin]] as ''Tertia Struthionum'', meaning ''the third of the ostriches''.<ref>{{cite journal
 | last=Knobel | first=E. B.
 | title=Al Achsasi Al Mouakket, on a catalogue of stars in the Calendarium of Mohammad Al Achsasi Al Mouakket
 | journal=[[Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society]]
 | volume=55 | issue=8
 | page=429 |date=June 1895
 | bibcode=1895MNRAS..55..429K | doi=10.1093/mnras/55.8.429| doi-access=free}}</ref> This star, along with [[Eta Ceti|η Cet]] (Deneb Algenubi), [[Theta Ceti|θ Cet]] (Thanih Al Naamat), [[Zeta Ceti|ζ Cet]] (Baten Kaitos), and [[Upsilon Ceti|υ Cet]], were ''Al Naʽāmāt'' (النعامات), the Hen Ostriches.<ref>{{cite book | last=Allen | first=R. H. | date=1963 | author-link=Richard Hinckley Allen | title=Star Names: Their Lore and Meaning | url=https://archive.org/details/starnamestheirlo00alle/page/162 | access-date=2010-12-12 | edition=Reprint | publisher=Dover Publications Inc. | location=New York | isbn=((0-486-21079-0)) | page=[https://archive.org/details/starnamestheirlo00alle/page/162 162] | url-access=registration }}</ref><ref>[[Eta Ceti|η Cet]] as '''Aoul al Naamat''' or '''Prima Sthrutionum''' (the first of the ostriches), [[Theta Ceti|θ Cet]] as '''Thanih al Naamat''' or '''Secunda Sthrutionum'''  (the second of the ostriches), τ Cet as '''Thalath al Naamat''' or '''Tertia Sthrutionum'''  (the third of the ostriches), and [[Zeta Ceti|ζ Cet]] as '''Rabah al Naamat''' or '''Quarta Sthrutionum'''  (the fourth of the ostriches). [[Upsilon Ceti|υ Cet]] should be '''Khamis al Naamat''' or '''Quinta Sthrutionum''' (the fifth of the ostriches) consistently, but Al Achsasi Al Mouakket designated the title ''the fifth of the ostriches'' to [[Gamma Andromedae|γ And]] with uncleared consideration.</ref>

In [[Chinese astronomy]], the "[[Bond (Chinese constellation)|Square Celestial Granary]]" ({{lang-zh|c=天倉|p=Tiān Cāng}}) refers to an [[asterism (astronomy)|asterism]] consisting of τ Ceti, [[Iota Ceti|ι Ceti]], [[Eta Ceti|η Ceti]], [[Zeta Ceti|ζ Ceti]], [[Theta Ceti|θ Ceti]] and [[57 Ceti]].<ref>{{cite book
 | script-title=zh:中國星座神話
 | author    = 陳久金
 | publisher = 台灣書房出版有限公司
 | date      = 2005
 | isbn      = 978-986-7332-25-7| language  = zh
 }}</ref> Consequently, the [[Chinese star names|Chinese name]] for τ Ceti itself is "the Fifth Star of Square Celestial Granary" ({{lang-zh|c=天倉五|p=Tiān Cāng wǔ|l=|links=no}}).<ref>{{cite web
 |url          = http://aeea.nmns.edu.tw/2006/0607/ap060710.html
 |script-title = zh:天文教育資訊網
 |trans-title  = Activities of Exhibition and Education in Astronomy (AEEA)
 |editor       = 陳輝樺
 |date         = July 10, 2006
 |language     = zh
 |access-date  = December 13, 2010
 |archive-date = July 16, 2011
 |archive-url  = https://web.archive.org/web/20110716123113/http://aeea.nmns.edu.tw/2006/0607/ap060710.html
 |url-status   = dead
}}</ref>

==Motion==
The [[proper motion]] of a star is its [[angular rate]] of movement across the [[celestial sphere]], determined by comparing its position relative to more distant background objects. Moving at {{val|1.9}} [[arcseconds]] per year ({{val|1900}} years per degree),<ref group="nb">The net proper motion is given by <math>\mu = \sqrt{\mu_\delta^2 + \mu_\alpha^2 \cdot \cos^2 \delta} = 1907.79~\text{mas/y}</math>, where ''μ<sub>α</sub>'' and ''μ<sub>δ</sub>'' are the components of proper motion in the RA and declination respectively, and ''δ'' is the declination. See: {{cite web | last = Majewski | first = Steven R. | date = 2006 | url = http://www.astro.virginia.edu/class/majewski/astr551/lectures/VELOCITIES/velocities.html | title = Stellar Motions | publisher = University of Virginia | access-date = 2007-09-27 | url-status = dead | archive-url = https://archive.today/20120125061201/http://www.astro.virginia.edu/class/majewski/astr551/lectures/VELOCITIES/velocities.html | archive-date = 2012-01-25 }}</ref> Tau Ceti is considered to be a high-proper-motion star. A high proper motion is an indication of closeness to the Sun:<ref>{{cite web
 | last=Reid | first=Neill | date=February 23, 2002
 | url=http://www-int.stsci.edu/~inr/nstars2.html
 | title=Meeting the neighbours: NStars and 2MASS
 | publisher=Space Telescope Science Institute
 | access-date=2006-12-11 }}</ref> nearby stars can traverse an angle of arc across the sky more rapidly than the distant background stars and are thus good candidates for [[parallax]] studies. In the case of Tau Ceti, the parallax measurements indicate a distance of {{val|11.9|ul=ly}}. This makes it [[List of nearest stars|one of the closest]] star systems to the Sun and the next-closest [[Stellar classification#Class G|spectral class-G star]] after [[Alpha Centauri|Alpha Centauri A]].<ref>{{cite web
 | last=Henry | first=Todd J.
 | date=October 1, 2006
 | url=http://www.chara.gsu.edu/RECONS/TOP100.htm
 | title=The One Hundred Nearest Star Systems
 | publisher=Research Consortium on Nearby Stars
 | access-date=2006-12-11 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20061128221321/http://www.chara.gsu.edu/RECONS/TOP100.htm| archive-date = November 28, 2006}}</ref>

[[File:Angular map of fusors around Sol within 12ly (large).png|thumb|Radar distance and position map of [[Nearest stars|the nearest star system]]s, stars or [[brown dwarf]]s within 12 light-years from Earth, with Tau Ceti marked at the edge of the map.]]

The [[radial velocity]] of a star is the component of its motion that is toward or away from the Sun. It can be determined by measuring the star's [[stellar spectrum|spectrum]]: due to the [[Doppler effect|Doppler shift]], the [[absorption line]]s in the spectrum of a star will be shifted slightly toward the red (or longer wavelengths) if the star is moving away from the observer, or toward blue (or shorter wavelengths) when it moves toward the observer. In the case of Tau Ceti, the radial velocity is about −17&nbsp;km/s, with the negative value indicating that it is moving toward the Sun.<ref>{{cite journal
 | author=Butler, R. P.
 | author2=Marcy, G. W.
 | author3=Williams, E.
 | author4=McCarthy, C.
 | author5=Dosanjh, P.
 | author6=Vogt, S. S.
 | title=Attaining Doppler Precision of 3 M s-1
 | journal=[[Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific]]
 | date=1996 | volume=108 | pages=500
 | bibcode=1996PASP..108..500B  | doi = 10.1086/133755
| doi-access=free
 }}</ref> The star will make its closest approach to the Sun in about 43,000&nbsp;years, when it comes to within {{Convert|3.25|pc|ly|order=flip|abbr=on}}.<ref name=aa575_A35>{{citation
 | last1=Bailer-Jones | first1=C. A. L.
 | title=Close encounters of the stellar kind
 | journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics
 | volume=575 | id=A35 | pages=13 | date=March 2015
 | doi=10.1051/0004-6361/201425221 | doi-access=free
 | bibcode=2015A&A...575A..35B
 | postscript=. |arxiv = 1412.3648 | s2cid=59039482
 }} (Data for Tau Ceti is in the CDS)</ref>

The distance to Tau Ceti, along with its proper motion and radial velocity, together give the motion of the star through space. The [[stellar kinematics|space velocity]] relative to the Sun is {{val|37.2|u=km/s}}.<ref name=Anderson2012>{{citation
 | title=XHIP: An extended hipparcos compilation
 | last1=Anderson | first1=E. | last2=Francis | first2=Ch.
 | journal=Astronomy Letters
 | volume=38 | issue=5 | pages=331 | year=2012
 | bibcode=2012AstL...38..331A | doi=10.1134/S1063773712050015
 | arxiv=1108.4971 | s2cid=119257644 | postscript=. }}</ref> This result can then be used to compute an orbital path of Tau Ceti through the [[Milky Way]]. It has a mean galacto-centric distance of {{val|9.7|u=kiloparsecs}} ({{val|32000|ul=ly}}) and an [[orbital eccentricity]] of 0.22.<ref name=Mello>{{cite journal
 | author=Porto de Mello, G. F.
 | author2=del Peloso, E. F.
 | author3=Ghezzi, L.
 | title=Astrobiologically interesting stars within 10 parsecs of the Sun
 | journal=[[Astrobiology (journal)|Astrobiology]] | date=2006 | volume=6
 | issue=2 | pages=308–331
 | doi= 10.1089/ast.2006.6.308
 | pmid=16689649 | bibcode=2006AsBio...6..308P|arxiv = astro-ph/0511180 | s2cid=119459291
 }}</ref>

==Physical properties==
[[Image:Compare sun tau ceti.png|left|thumb|320px|The Sun (left) is both larger and somewhat hotter than the less active Tau Ceti (right).]]
The Tau Ceti system is believed to have only one stellar component. A dim optical companion has been observed with magnitude 13.1. As of 2000, it was {{val|137|ul=arcseconds}} distant from the primary. It may be gravitationally bound, but it is considered more likely to be a line-of-sight coincidence.<ref name=kaler_stars_tauceti>{{cite web|last1=Kaler|first1=James|title=Tau Ceti|url=http://stars.astro.illinois.edu/sow/taucet.html|website=Stars|publisher=University of Illinois|access-date=27 July 2015}}</ref><ref name=wds>{{cite web|title=00-06 hour section|url=http://ad.usno.navy.mil/wds/Webtextfiles/wdsnewframe1.html|archive-url=https://archive.today/20110816042149/http://ad.usno.navy.mil/wds/Webtextfiles/wdsnewframe1.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=August 16, 2011|website=Washington Double Star Catalog|publisher=United States Naval Observatory|access-date=27 July 2015}}</ref><ref name=Pijpers2003>{{cite journal
 | last1=Pijpers | first1=F. P.
 | last2=Teixeira | first2=T. C.
 | last3=Garcia | first3=P. J.
 | last4=Cunha | first4=M. S.
 | last5=Monteiro | first5=M. J. P. F. G.
 | last6=Christensen-Dalsgaard | first6=J. | author-link6=Jørgen Christensen-Dalsgaard
 | date=2003 | title= Interferometry and asteroseismology: The radius of τ Ceti
 | journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics | volume=401 | issue=1 | pages= L15–L18
 | doi= 10.1051/0004-6361:20030837 | bibcode=2003A&A...406L..15P| doi-access=free }}</ref>

Most of what is known about the physical properties of Tau Ceti and its system has been determined through [[spectroscopy|spectroscopic]] measurements. By comparing the spectrum to computed models of [[stellar evolution]], the age, mass, radius and luminosity of Tau Ceti can be estimated. However, using an [[astronomical interferometer]], measurements of the radius of the star can be made directly to an accuracy of 0.5%.<ref name="aaa494">{{cite journal
 | display-authors=7
 | author=Teixeira, T. C.
 | author2=Kjeldsen, H.
 | author3=Bedding, T. R.
 | author3-link= Tim Bedding
 | author4=Bouchy, F.
 | author5=Christensen-Dalsgaard, J.
 | author5-link=Jørgen Christensen-Dalsgaard
 | author6=Cunha, M. S.
 | author7=Dall, T.
 | author8=Frandsen, S.
 | author9=Karoff, C.
 | author10=Monteiro, M. J. P. F. G.
 | author11=Pijpers, F. P.
 | title=Solar-like oscillations in the G8 V star τ Ceti
 | journal=Astronomy and Astrophysics | issue=1
 |date=January 2009 | pages=237–242
 | doi=10.1051/0004-6361:200810746
  | doi-access=free| volume=494 | bibcode=2009A&A...494..237T|arxiv = 0811.3989 | s2cid=59353134
 }}</ref> Through such means, the radius of Tau Ceti has been measured to be {{val|80.1|0.8|u=%}} of the [[solar radius]].<ref name=Bustos2026/> This is about the size that is expected for a star with somewhat lower mass than the Sun.<ref name="aa426">{{cite journal
 | display-authors=7
 | author=Di Folco, E.
 | author2=Thévenin, F.
 | author3=Kervella, P.
 | author4=Domiciano de Souza, A.
 | author5=du Foresto
 | author6=V. Coudé
 | author7=Ségransan, D.
 | author8=Morel, P.
 | title=VLTI near-IR interferometric observations of Vega-Like Stars
 | journal=Astronomy and Astrophysics
 | date=2004 | volume=426
 | issue=2 | pages=601–617
 | bibcode=2004A&A...426..601D
 | doi=10.1051/0004-6361:20047189 | doi-access=free
 }}</ref>

===Rotation===

The [[rotation period]] for Tau Ceti was measured by periodic variations in the classic H and K absorption lines of singly [[ion]]ized [[calcium]] (Ca II). These lines are closely associated with surface [[magnetism|magnetic]] activity,<ref>{{cite web
 |url         = http://www.mtwilson.edu/hk/Overview/
 |title       = H-K Project: Overview of Chromospheric Activity
 |publisher   = Mount Wilson Observatory
 |access-date  = 2006-11-15
 |url-status     = dead
 |archive-url  = https://web.archive.org/web/20060831111152/http://www.mtwilson.edu/hk/Overview/
 |archive-date = 2006-08-31
}}</ref> so the period of variation measures the time required for the activity sites to complete a full rotation about the star. By this means the rotation period for Tau Ceti is estimated to be {{val|34|u=days}}.<ref name="ApJL457">{{cite journal
 | last1=Baliunas | first1=S.
 | last2 =Sokoloff | first2=D.
 | last3 =Soon | first3=W.
 | title=Magnetic Field and Rotation in Lower Main-Sequence Stars: an Empirical Time-dependent Magnetic Bode's Relation?
 | journal=[[Astrophysical Journal Letters]] | date=1996
 | volume=457
 | issue =2 | pages=L99
 | bibcode=1996ApJ...457L..99B | doi=10.1086/309891 | doi-access=free
 }}</ref> Due to the [[Doppler effect]], the [[Stellar rotation|rotation rate of a star]] affects the width of the absorption lines in the spectrum (light from the side of the star moving away from the observer will be shifted to a longer wavelength; light from the side moving towards the observer will be shifted toward a shorter wavelength). By analyzing the width of these lines, the rotational velocity of a star can be estimated. The projected rotation velocity for Tau Ceti is

:{{nowrap|''v''<sub>eq</sub> · sin ''i'' ≈ 1 km/s,}}

where ''v''<sub>eq</sub> is the velocity at the [[equator]], and ''i'' is the [[inclination]] angle of the [[Rotation around a fixed axis|rotation axis]] to the [[Line-of-sight propagation|line of sight]]. For a typical G8 star, the rotation velocity is about {{val|2.5|u=km/s}}. The relatively low rotational velocity measurements may indicate that Tau Ceti is being viewed from nearly the direction of its pole.<ref name=Gray1994>{{cite journal
 | author=Gray, D. F.
 | author2=Baliunas, S. L.
 | title=The activity cycle of tau Ceti
 | journal=[[Astrophysical Journal]]
 | date=1994 | volume=427 | issue=2 | pages=1042–1047
 | bibcode=1994ApJ...427.1042G
 | doi = 10.1086/174210 | doi-access=free
 }}
</ref><ref>{{cite journal
 | author = Hall, J. C.
 | author2 = Lockwood, G. W.
 | author3 = Gibb, E. L.
 | title=Activity cycles in cool stars. 1: Observation and analysis methods and case studies of four well-observed examples
 | journal=Astrophysical Journal
 | date=1995 | volume=442 | issue=2 | pages=778–793
 | bibcode=1995ApJ...442..778H
 | doi=10.1086/175483 }}</ref>

More recently, a 2023 study has estimated a rotation period of {{val|46|4|u=days}} and a ''v''<sub>eq</sub> sin ''i'' of {{val|0.1|0.1|u=km/s}}, corresponding to a pole-on inclination of {{val|7|7|u=deg}}.<ref name="Korolik2023"/>

===Metallicity===

The chemical composition of a star provides important clues to its evolutionary history, including the age at which it formed. The [[interstellar medium]] of dust and gas from which stars form is primarily composed of [[hydrogen]] and [[helium]] with trace amounts of heavier elements. As nearby stars continually evolve and die, they seed the interstellar medium with an increasing portion of heavier elements. Thus younger stars tend to have a higher portion of heavy elements in their atmospheres than do the older stars. These heavy elements are termed "metals" by astronomers, and the portion of heavy elements is the [[metallicity]].<ref>{{cite journal
 | author=Carraro, G.
 | author2=Ng, Y. K.
 | author3=Portinari, L.
 | title=Age Metallicity Relation and Star Formation History of the Galactic Disk
 | journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society
 | date=1999 | volume=296 | issue=4 | pages=1045–1056
 | bibcode=1998MNRAS.296.1045C
 | doi=10.1046/j.1365-8711.1998.01460.x | doi-access=free
 |arxiv = astro-ph/9707185 | s2cid=14071760
 }}</ref> The amount of metallicity in a star is given in terms of the ratio of [[iron]] (Fe), an easily observed heavy element, to hydrogen. A [[logarithm]] of the relative iron abundance is compared to the Sun. In the case of Tau Ceti, the atmospheric metallicity is

:<math>\left[\frac{\text{Fe}}{\text{H}}\right] \approx -0.50</math>&nbsp;[[Decimal exponent|dex]],

equivalent to about a third the solar abundance. Past measurements have varied from −0.13 to −0.60.<ref name="strobel">{{cite journal
 | display-authors=7
 | author=de Strobel
 | author2=G. Cayrel
 | author3=Hauck, B.
 | author4=François, P.
 | author5=Thevenin, F.
 | author6=Friel, E.
 | author7=Mermilliod, M.
 | author8=Borde, S.
 | title=A catalogue of Fe/H determinations
 | edition=1991
 | journal=Astronomy and Astrophysics Supplement Series
 | date=1991 | volume=95 | issue=2 | pages=273–336
 | bibcode=1992A&AS...95..273C }}</ref><ref name="mnras286">{{cite journal
 | author=Flynn, C.
 | author2=Morell, O.
 | title=Metallicities and kinematics of G and K dwarfs
 | journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society
 | date=1997 | volume=286 | issue=3 | pages=617–625
 | bibcode=1997MNRAS.286..617F |arxiv = astro-ph/9609017 | doi=10.1093/mnras/286.3.617| doi-access=free
 | s2cid=15818154
 }}</ref>

This lower abundance of iron indicates that Tau Ceti is almost certainly older than the Sun. Its age had previously been estimated to be {{val|5.8|ul=Gyr}}, but is now thought to be around {{val|9|u=Gyr}}.<ref name="TG11"/> This compares with {{val|4.57|u=Gyr}} for the Sun. However, age estimates for Tau Ceti can range from 4.4 to {{val|12|u=Gyr}}, depending on the model adopted.<ref name="aa426" />

Besides rotation, another factor that can widen the absorption features in the spectrum of a star is [[pressure broadening]]. The presence of nearby particles affects the radiation emitted by an individual particle. So the line width is dependent on the surface pressure of the star, which in turn is determined by the temperature and surface gravity. This technique was used to determine the surface gravity of Tau Ceti. The {{nowrap|log ''g''}}, or logarithm of the star's surface gravity, is about 4.38,<ref name=Bustos2026/> very close to the {{nowrap|log ''g'' {{=}} 4.44}} for the Sun.<ref name="strobel" />

===Luminosity and variability===
The [[luminosity]] of Tau Ceti is equal to only 55% of the [[solar luminosity|Sun's luminosity]].<ref name=Mello /> A [[terrestrial planet]] would need to orbit this star at a distance of about {{val|0.7|ul=AU}} to match the solar [[insolation]] level of Earth, where an AU is the distance of the Earth from the Sun. This is approximately the same as the average distance between [[Venus]] and the Sun.

The [[chromosphere]] of Tau Ceti—the portion of a star's atmosphere just above the light-emitting [[photosphere]]—currently displays little or no magnetic activity, indicating a stable star.<ref>{{cite journal
 | author=Frick, P.
 | author2=Baliunas, S. L.
 | author3=Galyagin, D.
 | author4=Sokoloff, D.
 | author5=Soon, W. | title=Wavelet Analysis of Stellar Chromospheric Activity Variations
 | journal=The Astrophysical Journal
 | date=1997 | volume=483 | issue=1 | pages=426–434
 | doi = 10.1086/304206 | bibcode=1997ApJ...483..426F| doi-access=free
 }}</ref> One 9-year study of temperature, [[Granule (solar physics)|granulation]], and the chromosphere showed no systematic variations; Ca II emissions around the H and K [[Infrared astronomy|infrared bands]] show a possible 11-year cycle, but this is weak relative to the Sun.<ref name="Gray1994"/> Alternatively it has been suggested that the star could be in a low-activity state analogous to a [[Maunder Minimum]]—a historical period, associated with the [[Little Ice Age]] in Europe, when [[sunspot]]s became exceedingly rare on the Sun's surface.<ref>{{cite journal
 | author = Judge, P. G.
 | author2 = Saar, S. H.
 | date = July 18, 1995 | title = The outer solar atmosphere during the Maunder Minimum: A stellar perspective
 | journal = The Astrophysical Journal
 | volume = 663
 | issue = 1
 | pages = 643–656
 | publisher = High Altitude Observatory
 | bibcode= 2007ApJ...663..643J |doi = 10.1086/513004 | doi-access = free
 }}
</ref><ref>{{cite journal
 | author =Judge, Philip G.
 | author2 =Saar, Steven H.
 | author3 =Carlsson, Mats
 | author4 =Ayres, Thomas R. | title = A Comparison of the Outer Atmosphere of the "Flat Activity" Star τ Ceti (G8 V) with the Sun (G2 V) and α Centauri A (G2 V) | journal = The Astrophysical Journal
 | volume = 609 | issue = 1 | pages = 392–406 | date = 2004
 | doi = 10.1086/421044 | bibcode=2004ApJ...609..392J| doi-access = free}}</ref> [[Spectral line]] profiles of Tau Ceti are extremely narrow, indicating low turbulence and observed rotation.<ref>{{cite journal
 | author = Smith, G.
 | author2 = Drake, J. J.
 |date=July 1987 | title = The wings of the calcium infrared triplet lines in solar-type stars
 | journal = Astronomy and Astrophysics
 | volume = 181 | issue = 1 | pages = 103–111
 | bibcode = 1987A&A...181..103S }}</ref> The star's asteroseismological oscillations have an amplitude about half that of the Sun and a lower mode lifetime.<ref name="aaa494"/>

==Search for planets {{anchor|Planetary system}}==
The principal factors driving research interest in Tau Ceti are its proximity, its Sun-like characteristics, and the implications for possible life on its planets. For categorization purposes, Hall and Lockwood report that "the terms 'solarlike star', '[[solar analog]]', and 'solar twin' [are] progressively restrictive descriptions".<ref>{{cite journal
 | last1=Hall | first1=J. C.
 | last2=Lockwood | first2=G. W.
 | title=The Chromospheric Activity and Variability of Cycling and Flat Activity Solar-Analog Stars
 | journal=The Astrophysical Journal | date=2004
 | volume=614
 | issue=2 | pages=942–946
 | doi = 10.1086/423926
| bibcode=2004ApJ...614..942H| doi-access=free
 }}</ref>

In 1988, radial-velocity observations ruled out any periodical variations attributable to massive planets around Tau Ceti inside of Jupiter-like distances.<ref name="apj331">{{cite journal
 | last1=Campbell| first1= Bruce
 | last2=Walker | first2=G. A. H.  |date=August 1988
 | title=A Search for Substellar Companions to Solar-Type Stars
 | journal=Astrophysical Journal | volume=331| pages=902–921 |doi=10.1086/166608 | bibcode=1988ApJ...331..902C| doi-access=free}}</ref><ref name=EPE/> Ever more precise measurements continue to rule out such planets, at least until December 2012.<ref name=EPE>{{cite encyclopedia
 |title       = Tables of Stars monitored by spectroscopy, with NO planet found
 |access-date  = 2007-09-28
 |url         = http://exoplanet.eu/list_no.html
 |url-status     = dead
 |archive-url  = https://web.archive.org/web/20071012151613/http://exoplanet.eu/list_no.html
 |encyclopedia       = [[Extrasolar Planets Encyclopaedia]]
 |archive-date = 2007-10-12
}}</ref> The velocity precision reached is about 11&nbsp;m/s measured over a 5-year time span.<ref name=Endl2002>{{cite journal
 | last1=Endl | first1=M.
 | last2=Kurster | first2=M. | last3=Els | first3=S.
 | title=The planet search program at the ESO Coud´e Echelle spectrometer
 | journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics
 | date=2002 | volume=392
 | issue=2 | pages=585–594
 | bibcode=2002A&A...392..671E
 | doi=10.1051/0004-6361:20020937  | doi-access=free|arxiv = astro-ph/0207512 | s2cid=17393347
 }}</ref> This result excludes [[hot Jupiter]]s and probably excludes any planets with minimal mass greater than or equal to Jupiter's mass and with orbital periods less than 15 years.<ref name=Walker1995>{{cite journal
 | last1=Walker | first1=Gordon A. H.
 | last2=Walker | first2=Andrew H.
 | last3=Irwin | first3=Alan W. |display-authors= etal
 | title=A Search for Jupiter-Mass Companions to Nearby Stars
 | journal=Icarus | date=1995 | volume=116
 | issue=2
 | pages=359–375
 | bibcode=1995Icar..116..359W
 | doi=10.1006/icar.1995.1130 }} However, this does not exclude the possibility of a large planet with a mass greater than Jupiter's and an [[orbital plane (astronomy)|orbital plane]] that is nearly perpendicular to the line of sight.</ref> In addition, a survey of nearby stars by the [[Hubble Space Telescope]]'s [[Wide Field and Planetary Camera]] was completed in 1999, including a search for faint companions to Tau Ceti; none were discovered to limits of the telescope's resolving power.<ref name=Schroeder2000>{{cite journal
 | last1=Schroeder | first1=D. J.
 | last2=Golimowski | first2=D. A.
 | last3=Brukardt | first3=R. A.
 | display-authors=etal
 | title=A Search for Faint Companions to Nearby Stars Using the Wide Field Planetary Camera 2
 | journal=Astronomical Journal | date=2000 | volume=119
 | issue=2 | pages=906–922
 | doi = 10.1086/301227 | bibcode=2000AJ....119..906S| doi-access=free
 }}</ref>

However, these searches only excluded larger [[brown dwarf]] bodies and closer orbiting giant planets, so smaller, Earth-like planets in orbit around the star were not precluded.<ref name=Schroeder2000/> If hot Jupiters were to exist in close orbit, they would likely disrupt the star's [[habitable zone]]; their exclusion was thus considered positive for the possibility of Earth-like planets.<ref name="apj331"/><ref name=SolStation>{{cite web
 | url=http://www.solstation.com/stars/tau-ceti.htm
 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20000816230203/http://www.solstation.com/stars/tau-ceti.htm
 | url-status=dead
 | archive-date=August 16, 2000
 | title= Tau Ceti | access-date=2007-09-25
 | publisher=Sol Company }}</ref> General research has shown a positive correlation between the presence of planets and a relatively high-metallicity parent star, suggesting that stars with lower metallicity such as Tau Ceti have a lower chance of having planets.<ref>{{cite journal
 | last=Gonzalez | first=G. | date=March 17–21, 1997
 | title=The Stellar Metallicity – Planet Connection
 | journal=Brown Dwarfs and Extrasolar Planets
 | volume=134
 | pages=431
 | publisher=ASP Conference Series
 | bibcode=1998ASPC..134..431G }}</ref>

